        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "27c09d87-8b35-5109-afdc-98f698f761a6",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Peter Boyle, Danny Arnold, Michael Cavanaugh, Evan Lurie, Tané McClure\n- Works: Joe Bash, Barney Miller, The X-Files, Joe, The West Wing, Starman, Hunter, Monk, 24, Inferno\n- Organizations: New York City Police Department\n\nFacts:\n- In 1970, Peter Boyle had a breakthrough performance in the film \"Joe.\"\n- \"Joe Bash\" was created by veteran TV producer Danny Arnold after his successful New York City police detective sitcom \"Barney Miller.\"\n- In \"Joe Bash,\" Peter Boyle starred as a weary and embittered New York City Police Department beat cop.\n- In 1996, Peter Boyle won an Emmy Award for a guest-starring role on the science-fiction drama \"The X-Files.\"\n- Michael Cavanaugh's television guest credits include \"The West Wing,\" \"Joe Bash,\" \"Starman,\" \"Hunter,\" \"Monk,\" and \"24.\"\n- In the 1997 film \"Inferno,\" Evan Lurie, Michael Cavanaugh, and Tané McClure appear in other pivotal roles.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "631a7b5a-6b87-5996-90e9-2372d2cd8f9e",
        "text": "Page: Joe Bash. Sentence: Starring Peter Boyle as a weary and embittered New York City Police Department beat cop, it was created by veteran TV producer Danny Arnold following his successful New York City police detective sitcom \"Barney Miller\".\nPage: Peter Boyle. Sentence: Boyle, who won an Emmy Award in 1996 for a guest-starring role on the science-fiction drama \"The X-Files\", won praise in both comedic and dramatic parts following his breakthrough performance in the 1970 film \"Joe\".\nPage: Michael Cavanaugh (actor). Sentence: His television guest-credits include \"The West Wing\", \"Joe Bash\", \"Starman\", \"Hunter\", \"Monk\" and \"24\".\nPage: Inferno (1997 film). Sentence: Evan Lurie, Michael Cavanaugh and Tané McClure appear in other pivotal roles.",
